    {
      "id": "tracking",
      "chapter": "The privacy question",
      "narration": "Not every cookie comes from the site you are visiting. Many pages carry ads from an outside advertiser. That advertiser can set its own cookie, called a third-party cookie. When the same advertiser appears on many sites, it sees the same ID each time. It can follow your footprints from site to site and build a profile of your interests.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 9,
          "size": 6.5,
          "text": "Cookies from someone else",
          "draw": 0.8,
